The original steady model has conductances `(a,b,c)=(2,1,1)` W/K and input `(10,0)` W, giving temperature rises `(4,2)` K, ambient flows `(8,2)` W and internal flow `2` W. Moving the 10 W input to the second node gives `(2,6)` K and internal flow `−4` W. Elimination derives `D=ab+ac+bc`, and the nonnegative quadratic proves uniqueness for anchored components. Unanchored compatible inputs determine a difference but leave a common offset; incompatible net input admits no steady state. The single-node transient is a separate hypothetical model, not a claimed reduction of the two-node network. For `C=12 J/K`, `G=3 W/K`, `P=6 W` and zero initial rise, `θ(t)=2(1−exp(−t/4)) K`; at four seconds, the stored/released energies are `24(1−exp(−1))` and `24 exp(−1)` J. Derivation covers initial state, monotonic response, equilibrium, integrated energy and the separately solved zero-conductance limit.

Three hypothetical alternatives separate capacity and conductance decisions. B alone passes nominal temperature/time/resource constraints, but the explicit input/conductance hard bounds produce worst rise `63/38 K` and time constant `60/19 s`, both outside the required `1.6 K` and `3 s`. Covering those bounds requires minimum conductance `4 W/K`, or nominal at least `80/19 W/K` under a ±5% bound. Resource and realization evidence for that revision remains unavailable. The lesson also proves why an unforced time constant identifies only `C/G` and specifies further observations needed to distinguish parameters and criticize spatial lumping. All examples are analytic/synthetic; no physical experiment or manufactured service is claimed.

