The progression connects an intended function to material state, reference geometry, empirical response, compatible deformation and evidence about a realized object. It distinguishes modulus, component stiffness and strength; specimen deformation and support movement; equilibrium, compatibility and constitutive information. From σ=N/A, ε=δ/L and the declared small uniaxial law σ=E(ε−ε₀), it derives k=EA/L and N=k(δ−g), with g=Lε₀. Parallel members have common extension, summed stiffness and stiffness-weighted free extension; series members share force and add compliance. The model excludes lateral motion, instability, slip, plasticity, creep and damage rather than claiming to validate those phenomena.

Fixed-mismatch integration gives U=ΣNᵢ²/(2kᵢ). A direct quadratic expansion proves the unique one-dimensional dead-load equilibrium, and the zero-total-force state can retain mismatch energy U₀. The identity U(P)=U₀+P²/(2K) separates stored energy from work during a later load ramp. Adding support compliance gives X=δ+C_sP and support energy C_sP²/2; the ramp work includes both changes. Changing free strain or material state would require a different energy account.

The original hypothetical pair uses L=0.5 m, A=10 mm² each, E₁=50 GPa, E₂=150 GPa and free strains 0 and 80×10⁻⁶. At P=400 N, its extension is 130 μm and member forces are 130 and 270 N. Stresses are 13 and 27 MPa; total strain is 260×10⁻⁶ in both, while mechanical strains differ. At P=0, extension is 30 μm, forces are +30 and −30 N and energy is 0.0006 J. At 400 N, member energy is 0.0206 J. Support compliance 1/(8×10⁶) m/N adds 50 μm movement and 0.0100 J stored energy. Total movement is 180 μm and ramp work is 0.0300 J, distinguished from 0.0306 J final energy.

A rival doubles both member stiffnesses and changes support compliance to 1/(4×10⁶) m/N. It has the same complete force–actuator-movement line but only 80 μm specimen extension at 400 N. A local gauge can distinguish the models; more observations of the same actuator quantity cannot. A hypothetical input box has exact displacement limits 479/4,400,000 m and 37/240000 m (about 108.86–154.17 μm), so the nominal 130 μm result does not establish a 150 μm limit throughout that box. Derivative signs prove the endpoint bounds. Scaling both stiffnesses/bounds by 20% lowers the maximum to 97/720000 m (about 134.72 μm); changing section area is a modeled possibility whose realization and other requirements remain unverified. No material test, structure or production process was performed or certified.

