The original paper-board model distinguishes request acceptance, mode and physical movement with state (incoming, buffer, finished, pending, mode). It conserves two cards and has buffer capacity one. Commands are request/hold/cancel/recover/resume; a nondeterministic tick may idle or make one enabled movement. No elapsed time, event probability or guaranteed progress is supplied. Hold immediately suppresses movements in the model, but is explicitly not physical isolation or removal of stored energy. A general argument shows how each known held state reaches a resumable state in at most two cancel/recover actions while preserving completed output; this does not prove the information or human capability is available.

Possible-state sets connect observation to action: a command is uniformly admissible only if its precondition holds in every remaining state. Propagation after an event is separated from filtering by a fresh observation. After a request and an unobserved tick, incoming/pending and buffered/no-pending alternatives require different recovery actions. Counts-only handover misses a second pending-work ambiguity. Across all sixteen valid/reachable states, the minimal field selections determining the permitted-command set are mode, pending and any two of the three conserved counts. Conservation gives the omitted count. These mathematical results depend on exact current observations and the stated model; they do not prescribe a universal handover form or measure human error.

