The seventh engineering block is installed after `@engineering-feedback-review`. Its exact continuation is `engineering-production-boundary`, `engineering-production-visits`, `engineering-production-timing`, `engineering-production-scheduling`, `engineering-production-rework`, `engineering-production-inventory-time`, `engineering-production-decision`, `engineering-production-study`, `engineering-production-review`. Engineering now has **75 common learning Records**. Requirements, the earlier card-service and 100/80 entrance examples, port balances, network studies, discrete counting, concurrency and inquiry reporting are reused by reference. The inventory lesson links the shared integral definition explicitly. Existing shared texts, programs, identities and selected continuations remain unchanged; production and clean manufacturing are not silently imported into the common selection.

The beginning names useful accepted output and a system/time boundary, distinguishes released pieces, visits, WIP, final rejects/scrap and rework, and explains why a piece count is not a universal mass balance. Product evidence, actual quality and stipulated model acceptance labels remain separate. Production stays inside engineering; clean manufacturing remains its deferred specialization. The earlier aggregate-capacity study is not repeated as if new.

Original finite model: at most four distinguishable jobs, each with AB or ABAB route and a fixed final acceptance label; at most eight slots of binary A/B availability; waiting capacities K_A,K_B between zero and four. Phases count completed operations. Raw and departed items are outside the waiting buffers; unfinished even phases wait for A and odd phases for B. Both actions use the start state, each operation takes the whole slot and completions occur at its end. Destination space is reserved after simultaneous withdrawals. Same-slot chaining is forbidden. This timing makes K_B=0 stop A advancement and K_A=0 prevent rework return; the text explicitly does not transfer those results to real zero-buffer lines with direct handoff or machine-held work.

Visit accounting gives V_A=Σceil(p_j/2), V_B=Σfloor(p_j/2), V_A−V_B=Q_B, and each visit count is bounded by the corresponding available slots. Released=accepted+scrapped+unfinished is checked at every state. For accepted ABAB+AB jobs, three visits per machine are necessary. A six-slot trace with A available only in the first three slots and B only in the last three has those totals yet cannot complete the rework job. The independent schedule checker confirms only one accepted output: temporal precedence adds information absent from aggregate budgets.

The solver retains minimum accumulated item-time for each time/phase tuple. A slot contributes previous unfinished WIP plus new releases; items completing at the end count for the full slot. Prefix decomposition proves the recurrence, and state sufficiency proves dominance of the least-area history at a fixed state. The final lexicographic objective maximizes good output, then minimizes scrap, final WIP and area. Future route/quality/calendar knowledge makes this a perfect-information scenario optimum, not an online policy. Hidden setup or age-dependent quality would require an enlarged state and proof.

The original four-slot case has jobs 0=ABAB good, 1=AB good, 2=ABAB scrap; both machines always available and K_A=K_B=1. Schedule **(0,−),(1,0),(0,1),(−,0)** ends at phases **(4,2,0)** with two good items, no scrap/WIP, three visits per machine and item-time six. Only two jobs can be accepted, and their six operation durations impose area at least six; the witness attains both bounds. The busier **(0,−),(1,0),(2,1),(0,2)** performs seven operations, finishes one good item, leaves two unfinished and has area eight. Capacity (0,1) permits only one accepted item; (1,2) gives no better primary optimum than (1,1) in this scenario. Monotonicity of optimized primary output is proved by feasible-set inclusion under unchanged assumptions, not generalized to quality-changing designs or fixed heuristics.

The separate bounded-rework account uses conditional fractions f,r,g. Four exclusive paths sum to one; good fraction y=f+(1−f)rg, scrap 1−y, and visits per released item at each resource v=1+(1−f)r. For f=3/4,r=1,g=1/2, an exact completed eight-item batch gives seven good, one scrap and ten visits per machine. Useful output per raw item increases, while useful output per constrained visit falls from 3/4 to 7/10. Positive rework improves that latter ratio only when g>f. Expected-value interpretation, finite integer counts, ideal aggregate capacity and deadline guarantees have separate premises; no empirical defect distribution is asserted.

The inventory proof integrates presence indicators to obtain the exact clipped residence sum Σmax(0,min(d_i,v)−max(a_i,u)). Intervals (−3,1),(0,2),(3,7) on [0,5] give clipped lengths 1,2,2 and average WIP one; using completed departures with their untruncated mean instead gives 6/5. For initially empty, locally finite arrivals, A(T)=D(T)W_D(T)+R(T), with unfinished elapsed residence R. Empty-to-empty finite windows have R=0. The stated sufficient limit requires D/T→λ, W_D→W and R/T→0, with finite limits; arrival/departure rate equality requires its own net-inventory condition. D=0 is handled without an undefined mean. The four-slot optimum has area six, mean WIP 3/2, departure rate 1/2 and mean residence three.

